## Self-edit pass

Before handing off a draft, reread it top to bottom and edit in place. Goal:
one clean linear story — final claims stated directly, contradictions
resolved, open questions either answered or genuinely the user's. Improve the
draft itself; produce no separate cleanup note.

Find and fix:

- **Thought-process prose** — narrated deliberation ("I considered X, then
  realised Y"). Rewrite as what we will do.
- **Mid-stream reversals** — a paragraph that discovers and corrects its own
  error. Drop the wrong half; if which half is right is unclear, treat as a
  contradiction and investigate.
- **Contradictions between sections** — find which is true; rewrite both to
  agree. Do not hedge both.
- **Open questions you can answer** — anything reading code, checking a schema
  or running a query would settle is your work. Replace the question with the
  answer.
- **Approval requests dressed as open questions** — "confirm this direction",
  "approve X before implementation proceeds", "sign off that the earlier
  answer still holds", or anything whose only answers are "yes, go ahead" and
  "no, change it". Cut on sight: the document already goes to a user gate;
  approval is the workflow, not a question. If your reasoning eliminated every
  alternative, state the decision with the reasoning that forced it. If
  pruning empties the section, remove it.
- **Terminology drift** — one concept, two names. Normalise; if you cannot
  tell whether they mean the same thing, that is a contradiction to
  investigate.
- **Promised but missing content** — write it or drop the promise.
- **Speculative claims** — "I think this probably means…". Verify if it
  matters; cut if not.
- **Filler** — throat-clearing, context the reader already has,
  cross-references that earn nothing.

Preserve: all substantive technical content (never trim detail to shorten),
genuine user-judgement open questions, any note explaining what changed since
the user last read the document, and the domain's own terminology.
